#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""censused.py -- tables the `.ini` grammar census settled, per build.

**GENERATED by `tools/gen_ini_specs.py`. Do not hand-edit.** Regenerate with

    py -3 tools/gen_ini_specs.py --write

and `tests/test_censused.py` fails if this file drifts from what the installs
actually contain.

WHY IT EXISTS. The plugins hand-declared 3-5 `.ini` tables each while the
installs ship 74-216. An undeclared subject is not merely unread, it is
INVISIBLE: `comod catalogs` cannot refuse a table it has never heard of, so
the tool's silence about `3DMotion.ini` read exactly like its silence about a
file that does not exist.

WHAT IS IN HERE, AND WHAT IS NOT. Only files whose grammar the census settled
by EXACT CONSUMPTION -- every meaningful line accounted for. `partial` and
`mixed` files are deliberately absent: a spec asserts the build knows the
table's shape, and declaring an unsettled file would turn "not established"
into a row count. They stay in the census artefact and in
`ini_census.py --partials`.

THE LABEL KEYS WERE MEASURED. Each sections table was opened and its keys
counted; a key is declared only if it carries a non-empty value on at least
90% of sections. Where none does, the spec says `label_key=None`, which is
the declaration `NoSubjectPrintsAColumnOfBlanks` accepts -- "this table has no
label, the id stands alone". Inheriting the `"Name"` default instead is what
printed a blank column on six builds before that guard existed.

A HAND-WRITTEN SPEC ALWAYS WINS. Where a plugin already declares a subject,
the generated one is dropped: the hand-written one was authored by someone who
knew what the table MEANS, and this file knows only what shape it is in.
"""
